00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:36.320
So obviously it wasn't an option back then.

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:46.560
But it puts you into percentiles for how or how efficient you were or how good you were with your resources.

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:56.060
And I think this is like the precursor to that like kind of scoring of that like relativistic like this is how good you could have been sort of thing.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:18:02.100
And I think it's more it's a more definitive way than a high score, you know, than like a really large number.

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:09.880
This is a more definitive way to kind of like track your progress and see if you like played better than the previous time.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:16.660
Because I think like when you get such a large number like 138,000, you're like, what does that mean to me?

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:26.220
Yeah. You know, whereas like the GDV will actually tell you like, hey, you're doing better than your previous time because your score is two points higher.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:28.300
You know, like, oh, that's digestible.

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.120
Exactly. I'd like to see that in more games for sure.

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:39.280
To go back to the time limit thing, though, Joe, I don't think we're harping on that because I do think if I'm thinking about the things that took me out of the game the most,

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:46.220
it was that the later puzzles as they got harder and harder, I just did not have enough time to solve them.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:52.700
And I keep if I'm being honest, this is one of those games where I wasn't that great at it, but I kept booting it up because I wanted to enjoy it.

00:18:53.060 --> 00:19:01.920
And the time limit kept harping that it kept saying like, no, you're not going to be able to have a good time here because you're not going to have enough time to figure it out regardless.

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:05.500
Well, here's a quick thing about time limit.

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:09.740
I have a problem with it, too, but not not the time limit.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:21.100
The thing I don't like about how this game is structured is and I know that there is like a cheat sort of there's like a built in cheat code so that it doesn't do this.

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:33.140
But the fact that you only have lives and then you go back to square one, I think this is a game that should just respond to you at the beginning of the puzzle.

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:42.880
Because otherwise, so you're you're trying to use your your problem solving skills to to fix this one this one problem, this one puzzle.

00:19:43.260 --> 00:19:45.640
And now you're kicked back to level one.

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:47.780
You got to do all this stuff you probably memorized already.

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:48.880
You don't get a continue.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.320
Continue. I think you get.

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:51.560
I actually don't know.

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:52.700
I thought you get a continue.

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:54.480
But your GDV resets.

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:56.480
Is that is that the case?

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:58.280
I thought that was the case.

00:19:58.280 --> 00:19:59.440
I don't remember, to be honest.

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:03.440
OK, because whenever I lost my lives, I went back to one.

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:04.780
OK, maybe it's after.

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:06.580
Yeah, maybe. I think I did, too.

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:08.200
But OK, I remember.

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:09.420
It might be after a certain point.

00:20:09.620 --> 00:20:17.500
But my point, though, is that, like, I think that you should be able to keep attempting the level you're on because that's what you should be focused on.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:18.940
I completely agree. That's the problem you're trying to solve.

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:20.140
That's the kind of game this is.

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:23.140
Yeah. You don't care about playing this level again.

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:24.820
You already know the answer.

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:27.060
Or, I mean, you could play it and find a better way.

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:38.980
But, yeah. That's a problem because I think the game, as much as, you know, we described it as an action puzzle platformer, I think that the game doesn't know whether it wants to be a puzzle game or an action game.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:45.320
And I think that that's the weird thing about sending you back to level one makes you treat it more like a puzzle, more like an action game.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:51.260
Yeah. Where it's like anything can happen each time and it's up to you to, you know, use your skills and stuff.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:53.680
But I think it's more about a puzzle game.

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:56.320
Yeah. Where it's like, OK, but I've solved that puzzle.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:59.140
I'm not going to solve it differently now that I know how to solve it.

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:01.880
So you're basically just asking me to do these tasks again.

00:21:02.060 --> 00:21:03.440
Yeah. So I agree with that problem.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:06.140
It's definitely, I think it leans, it knows it's a puzzle game.

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:08.900
But it has all these weird holdovers from action games.

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:11.160
Like it has, like, who needs lives in a puzzle game?

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:12.780
I don't know. Yeah.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:19.220
I think it even would have been, you know, would have been fine if like, yeah, if you weren't losing your lives for these things.

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:22.460
If it was like you, your time limit runs out, fine.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.300
You can keep, you could still solve the puzzle and move on.

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:27.060
You just won't get those extra points from the leftover time.

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:31.280
Right. Like, so like, yeah, you could play and try and beat the time limit if that's what you want.

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:37.160
So there's 64 levels, 15 are secret and one is the final level.

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:39.800
Did anybody find any secret levels?

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:44.820
I know of one, but I didn't find it.

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:45.980
That's all that matters. Okay.

00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:46.860
So you know of one.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:48.300
Did you go in it? Did you see it?

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:53.980
I had to, this was when I was watching people play just to get more out of it.

00:21:54.020 --> 00:22:03.120
Yeah. Um, but if there is one of the first levels, there is just the Zodiac sign for, I think, um, the twins.

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:04.960
I don't remember which ones the twins are.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.300
Yeah. I don't know Zodiac signs at all.

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:14.740
I'm not the person to ask here, but yeah, there is a, basically, you know, there's a Zodiac sign for each, like every four levels.

00:22:14.960 --> 00:22:17.500
Yeah. There's one Zodiac sign attributed to it.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:20.160
So when you, uh, it just looks like a square.

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:22.500
It looks like just a part of the background of the level.

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:25.920
But if you go up to that square, you can collect that piece.

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:29.340
And then when you finish the level, you don't go to the next level.

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:31.100
You go to that Zodiac room.

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:36.100
Right. And, um, it's a, it's a little differently, um, designed.

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:39.460
Like it's mostly breakable and createable blocks.

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:40.360
Okay. This one.

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:42.420
Um, so I guess it's a bit more free form.

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:51.340
It's more of like a, like a room full of coins in Mario where there's a lot more, uh, rewards than there are, um, obstacles.

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:53.180
Joe, did you know about the secret rooms?

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:54.360
I didn't know about until just now.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:59.740
Yeah. There, this is considered just for the record guys, this is considered a very hard NES game.

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:04.080
Uh, like it's up there, you know, it doesn't have that legendary ghost and goblin status.

00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:08.800
Maybe it's just because less people would choose to play a game like this, but it's very hard.

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:16.800
And you know, there, there's like multiple endings kind of based on like how well you perform if you get all the way to the end.

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:19.100
And it's like to get to the end is one feed.

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:26.980
But then to find out that like, oh, I didn't collect all the fairies and oh, I didn't get to the final, you know, to the seat, to all the secret levels.

00:23:27.140 --> 00:23:29.840
It's like just even finding that stuff is crazy.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:37.120
There, there are things in this game where you will have to spawn a block in a specific place.

00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:42.500
Unspawn it just to get like, uh, you know, an item that you couldn't find elsewhere.

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:49.260
Yeah, it's so strange that like in, in empty space I was watching when it wasn't a speed runner, but it was somebody that was good at this game.

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:52.920
Just knowing where all of these like phantom blocks were.

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:54.980
Yeah. But here's my question.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:58.840
Do you guys think that that is a problem in this game?

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:02.500
Like the fact that there's like all of this secret stuff that you might never find.

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:11.940
Is that a problem? That kind of reminds me of like a, like a hidden block in Mario or granted Mario is usually designed where you probably will accidentally bump your head on the hidden block.

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:19.480
But, but really there's no like, you kind of get the feeling like, oh, I, I just happened to jump into that block and that, and it's there.

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:20.520
I wouldn't have known it was there.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:26.900
Yeah. So I mean, I do think as long as it's never like necessary, I think it'd be really cool to be like, I put a block there and I destroyed it.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:28.120
And there's like a hidden item under it.

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:29.320
I'm like, oh, cool. That was a secret.

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:34.620
In these, in the, in what I was looking at though, uh, this isn't, you have to have intent.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:37.900
Like it, you, it's not something that you're sort of out of the way.

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:39.740
It's not, yeah, it's not something you would do.

00:24:39.740 --> 00:24:41.900
That's my only argument against what Joe said.

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:43.460
Uh, but you know, you just didn't know.

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:47.000
Well, yeah. Is that like, it's, it's not done with intent here.

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:48.140
Uh, it's, it's.

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:50.020
Oh no, you have to have intent for it.

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:56.240
Oh, sorry. I guess what I meant was is that it doesn't, it wasn't designed in a way that it's like people will find this.

00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:57.640
It was meant to be hidden.

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:00.380
And I feel both ways about it.

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:09.720
So I think it's kind of dumb that it's not intuitive, but at the same time, if you, if this is the game that you own and you can like explore every nook and cranny of it,

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:16.200
it is kind of cool that there are these like, you know, items that you might never find just, just to give an idea.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:22.560
Like the guy who made the game facts, uh, FAQ for this, uh, he wrote it.

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:27.760
And then like, he came back like eight years later and was like, Hey guys, I was just checking out the game the other day.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:29.440
And I noticed like these other things.

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:31.440
So I added some extra things that I just found.

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:35.120
And it's like, wow, you just found new stuff for your guide.

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:36.840
I almost see.

00:25:36.940 --> 00:25:48.800
I almost wish then if there was a secret block up in the corner of one level, you know, you could, there would be some indicator, just like a platform or something where you could, you know, I've beaten a level.

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:51.640
I figured out a few different ways to do it, but like, why is that?

00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:53.580
I feel I can get up to that spot.

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:58.020
Why, why can I, I wonder if there's something up there that you can go up and explore around up there or something.

00:25:58.200 --> 00:25:59.160
You bring up a good point though.

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:03.120
Did you ever feel like the game was nudging you to do a certain thing?

00:26:03.420 --> 00:26:07.400
Like, or do you think that it presents the puzzle and says you figure it out?

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:11.840
I think that there are certain, uh, signifiers of what you should do.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:15.380
I think enemy placement, uh, is one of the biggest ones.

00:26:15.820 --> 00:26:25.800
Um, if you see some of those lightning guys just circling around one block, you might be, uh, asked to move them somewhere else.

00:26:25.980 --> 00:26:32.480
Right. Or, or there are those things that fall out of like the weirdly shaped holes in the wall that fall straight down.

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:36.480
Maybe you should, uh, make it so that they fall into a pit instead of going down a rope.

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:42.920
But also, yeah, I'll see them falling and they're not, they're not dangerous to me because they're like, they're hitting a platform that's blocking them.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:50.180
But then I realized like, oh, the game, I need to open that platform and let them out in order to get what I need.

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:53.860
Like, you know what I mean? Like, oh, they wouldn't put something dangerous up there unless I needed to get up there.

00:26:54.060 --> 00:27:05.920
Right. I think, you know, I'm just going to sound like a broken record here, but since we're talking about the secrets and the different ways that the game nudges you, it just, again, it feels very strange that we have a time limit.

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:08.100
Like, how am I ever going to be able to get a chance?

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:15.560
I was even just thinking about it as I was saying, like, oh, you get, you know, if this is the only game you own and you want to explore every nook and cranny, it's like you don't get time to explore every nook and cranny.

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:22.100
And there are a lot of cool, you know, if you had more time, there are a lot of cool alternate ways to approach things.

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:29.800
Like, there was, like, sometimes there's with those electrical guys that, like, they zoom along the perimeter of walls and of blocks and everything.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:39.120
There are sometimes ways that you can, like, box them closer and closer into each other without ever letting them on the other, like, from the other side of the wall.

00:27:39.320 --> 00:27:44.020
The way you keep creating and destroying blocks, you make sure they can never touch you.

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:47.080
You can just box them into a tiny little box and, like, make them.

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:49.540
It's like, really, it was kind of cool when I discovered that.

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:50.940
It reminded me of that game.

00:27:51.560 --> 00:28:00.060
It was on, like, Windows 95 where you were the cat or you were the mouse and you had to put the cats in the corner and get the cheese.

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:01.920
Yeah, sorry. All those Windows 95 games.

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:13.240
Nostalgia trip. But, yeah, just I feel like I found that one and I feel like there were probably tons of other, like, little tricks and secrets and just cool ways of doing things that, you know, that I would have liked to explore.

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:17.820
But I never felt like I had time to try and experiment with that stuff because just, like, no, got to get to the key.

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:21.700
Okay. Should levels be unwinnable?

00:28:22.420 --> 00:28:26.660
Should you fall into a point where you can no longer win the level?

00:28:27.540 --> 00:28:30.420
And, like, is that good game design for you?

00:28:30.420 --> 00:28:33.940
I think if you can fall into that point, the game should, the level should end.

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:35.040
You should have a ton.

00:28:35.220 --> 00:28:36.320
Oops, you're done.

00:28:36.740 --> 00:28:37.720
But I didn't notice.

00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:39.700
Kind of like a suicide of your character.

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:42.520
I'm imagining the Mario, like, and he falls.

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:48.440
Yeah, yeah, yeah. But I don't think I ran into a situation where I boxed myself completely out of the level.

00:28:48.640 --> 00:28:50.180
Like, there's always something I can undo.

00:28:50.440 --> 00:29:01.300
I don't think a level is ever unwinnable, but I think you certainly can get to a point where you've just unleashed too much on you and you'll just be in a constant state of trying to jump over obstacles.

00:29:01.540 --> 00:29:03.020
Like, you'll be just playing jump rope with the enemies.

00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:05.860
I guess if I would get to that point, I would just end up getting killed.

00:29:05.980 --> 00:29:07.400
That's what I'm saying, though. It felt like I got trapped.

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:14.740
It felt like I died. I do think that there are actually ways that you can break the level and that you just have to wait out the timer.

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:19.080
I don't think that that is a knock against the game.

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:22.540
I think that you have failed this puzzle.

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:25.800
And maybe it's a little cruel to make you wait it out.

00:29:26.680 --> 00:29:28.760
Well, you could jump down to your death.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:31.160
Or you could do that. Assuming it's one of those levels.

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:32.260
Not all of them have pits.

00:29:32.260 --> 00:29:41.800
No, no. But I think because there are even modern games that you can, like, fail just because you did something wrong.

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:47.780
Sure. But usually I would imagine they would then tell you and not make you just sit there and not know if you failed or not.

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:55.840
You know what I mean? I'm failing to come up with one right now, but I do know that they exist and you just have to, like, press R to restart or something like that.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:30:01.580
But if there was a way for you to just restart, that would work because it's a lives-based game.

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:04.740
Right. Then it would be a much easier pill to swallow.

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:09.560
How do we feel about using guides with puzzle games specifically?

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:15.680
I don't know. Personally, I think that the guide should be like, this is how you do this.

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:17.560
This is not how you solve the puzzle.

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:18.940
These are your tools.

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:20.220
This is how you use them. Things like that.

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:22.300
But I mean, I guess, yeah, it's really not much of a guide.

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:28.260
That's a good point, but I'm specifically here talking about, like, a guide that tells you how to solve the puzzle.

00:30:28.340 --> 00:30:29.840
How do you feel about using that?

00:30:30.020 --> 00:30:46.340
See, I'm kind of – I don't really like strategy guides to begin with unless they are more for, like, in creative games of, like, here are some ideas that you can use to optimize your city.

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:48.300
Yeah, like tips and tricks instead of – Yeah, tips and tricks.

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:50.060
I like tips and tricks.

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:55.440
I don't, like, walk me – take my hand and walk me through this game.

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:59.260
I don't mind that in certain types of games.

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:01.560
Puzzle games, it's a big no for me.

00:31:01.880 --> 00:31:03.400
That's cheating because that's the game.

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:05.420
Yeah, you've essentially, like, lost out.

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:10.800
You're not playing this game to have the accomplishment of putting the inputs in yourself.

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:13.260
The accomplishment is solving the puzzle.

00:31:13.300 --> 00:31:18.060
It's like you're playing the New York Times crossword from yesterday with today's paper.

00:31:18.180 --> 00:31:21.940
Right, exactly. Exactly, but just something that you reference when you get stuck.

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:25.420
You know, it's like – you're like, oh, I'm just going to check this one word.

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:32.080
It's like sooner or later than you wind up checking the whole thing anyway, and then it just doesn't become fun in the first place because you never earned it.

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:42.040
You know, I think, like, it's okay to use it in, like, you know, let's just say a Legend of Zelda style game, you know, where it's like, oh, I'm really stuck right now.

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:44.440
Yeah, I'm lost. I don't know what to do.

00:31:44.600 --> 00:31:49.580
Yeah, that makes sense. You know, that makes more sense, but you can't use it, you know, to the same point.

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:55.300
You can't use it in the Legend of Zelda during the middle of a dungeon because that's a puzzle that you have to solve.

00:31:55.460 --> 00:32:03.800
It's like – And personally, I'm always, like – I like to use that kind of thing as a last resort even if it's like – you know, I was playing – what is it?

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:11.000
It's Dishonored 2, and I was stuck, and I did not want to look up where to go because the part of the fun is exploring, but I'm stuck forever.

00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:13.960
So finally, I was like, okay, where do I – and it was just something I totally missed.

00:32:14.040 --> 00:32:18.360
But, like, usually I kind of don't like doing that because, like, the idea is, like, I'm trying to immerse myself.

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:19.360
Like, I'm this guy.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:21.180
I'm lost. I need to find my way out.

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:24.220
Totally, yeah. So, like, I don't like to use guides in general.

00:32:24.440 --> 00:32:32.980
And I can see that being a thing in Solomon's Key as well where it's like, you know, I've been stuck on this particular level for a very long time, and I just want to –

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:36.440
like, I'm not going to use the guide for the very next level, but I'm done with this one.

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:39.100
I do think that there is, like, a statute of limitations.

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:46.200
It's still – I mean, like, that is, like, if you do translate it to, like, a junkie saying, like, I see this one last fix.

00:32:46.340 --> 00:32:49.660
Like, you might – you'll probably get addicted to the strategy guide.

00:32:49.660 --> 00:32:52.420
That's why I say strategy guide is not even once for puzzle games.

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:58.100
I did not use a strategy guide for this one, and that did limit how far I was able to get into the game,

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:02.560
but I definitely didn't think I was going to get anything more out of the game by looking up a strategy guide and being like,

00:33:02.980 --> 00:33:04.920
oh, yeah, that's how you do it.

00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:08.560
Like, there was a part of me that I had to, like, work myself out of.

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:14.040
I had to start embracing the mistake in these kinds of games, but – and this is sort of a tangent –

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:17.780
but in, like, story-based games or decision-based games,

00:33:17.820 --> 00:33:21.960
and I know that, like, the ending of a lot of games doesn't change too much by your decisions,

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:27.820
but, like, in games that you interact with your party, I'll be like – they'll show me in, like, Mass Effect,

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:30.620
like, you can say this, that, or the other thing.

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:35.320
And I'm like, well, I don't want to say the wrong thing, so I'm like, what happens if I say this?

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:39.960
And I did that for a while, and it was ruining games for me.

00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:43.700
Especially those story-building games because now you're just –

00:33:43.700 --> 00:33:47.360
sure, you're getting the optimal story where, like, the best storyline possible,

00:33:47.540 --> 00:33:48.980
but it's not your story.

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:51.240
No, no, now it's just, like, it's the best thing ever.

00:33:51.400 --> 00:33:52.720
Yeah, it's the story you wanted, but it's not –

00:33:52.720 --> 00:33:55.000
It's not my story. At that point, you're watching a movie.

00:33:55.200 --> 00:33:57.740
Yeah. I got that way – not to go on a total –

00:33:57.740 --> 00:33:59.100
No, it's okay. This is a good discussion.

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:02.880
With Skyrim because Skyrim, it's, like, you know, it's role-playing.

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:05.560
I'm like, I want to play as this character, and he does this, this, and this,

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:07.500
but he has all these powers and whatever.

00:34:07.640 --> 00:34:10.720
And I was always trying to get those, like, I need to get these powers to play this character,

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:12.980
but I'm like, no, the whole point is, like, his journey.

00:34:13.100 --> 00:34:17.300
Like, I got to get there in a way where I don't know what's coming or else it's no fun.

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:20.520
I'm sure all of our viewers can relate Solomon's key to Skyrim.

00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:23.920
Right. Absolutely. We got there.

00:34:23.920 --> 00:34:25.400
One last question for you guys.

00:34:25.700 --> 00:34:27.780
Is this a puzzle game or an action game?

00:34:27.940 --> 00:34:30.500
It's a puzzle game. Yeah, it should be a puzzle game.

00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:33.080
It's a puzzle game with action problems.

00:34:33.380 --> 00:34:34.260
Yes, action problems.

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:35.600
I like that even better, okay?

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:44.040
To go back to the guide thing real fast, Saruta actually was in a guide-like situation.

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:46.960
He describes this after the game went on sale.

00:34:47.420 --> 00:34:52.560
Soon after Solomon's Key went on sale, a primary school teacher came and asked us questions

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:54.400
so they could finish the game.

00:34:54.640 --> 00:34:59.780
He wanted to show the ending of the game to his class for fun on the last day of school,

00:34:59.780 --> 00:35:04.940
but there was a level that posed them a lot of problems, and hence they wanted us to give

00:35:04.940 --> 00:35:05.780
them the solution.

00:35:05.780 --> 00:35:08.160
I was happy we could show him.

00:35:09.360 --> 00:35:14.700
So, in a way, he even helped gamers cheat his own game.

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:15.820
He was like, you know what? Yeah, it's fine.

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:17.140
I'll show you how to finish the game.

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:22.000
I love these stories of before these companies got so big that they didn't have hearts.

00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:23.880
Yeah. Just interacting with people.

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:24.940
Wait, random people.

00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:27.920
A child bought. A child who bought the Gradius series.

00:35:28.380 --> 00:35:32.700
And then this teacher who's like, yeah, I want to show my kids the ending.

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:34.560
And he's like, okay, here's the answer.

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:40.480
Yeah. Now you have to call into, well, not now, but eventually you'd have to call into Toys R Us

00:35:40.480 --> 00:35:41.540
for your answer. You know?

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:44.040
You couldn't get a hold of Shigeru Miyamoto.

00:35:44.380 --> 00:35:46.160
He'd be like, Miyamoto, what do I do?

00:35:46.160 --> 00:35:47.640
I don't have the sword.

00:35:47.640 --> 00:35:48.700
He's like, did you go in the cave?

00:35:49.540 --> 00:36:21.660
Did you go in the cave? Did you go in the cave?

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:32.680
All right. That's Solomon's Key for me.

00:36:32.740 --> 00:36:33.740
Any straight thoughts, guys?

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:36.820
Maybe something we should have talked about in general.

00:36:37.400 --> 00:36:41.160
It has that thing where you jump with the up button.

00:36:41.520 --> 00:36:43.440
This one didn't bother me, though.

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:47.380
I don't think it was too bothersome because you don't need to do quick jumping, Mario jumping,

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:48.560
but I still noticed it.

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:50.680
I don't know what the solution would have been.

00:36:50.680 --> 00:36:52.580
You kind of need the A and the B button otherwise.

00:36:52.680 --> 00:36:55.680
But it's just one of those things that it's like the NES controller, you're not going to

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:57.680
get better than that if you need A and B for something else.

00:36:57.800 --> 00:37:01.240
I think it was, here's the interesting thing, because it's that control scheme across the

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:02.740
board. Now, you guys know me lately.

00:37:02.740 --> 00:37:06.400
I've been getting kind of crazy, and I have to try the games on every system that they

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:15.460
came out on. So I tried Solomon's Key on the NES, the Commodore 64, the arcade, and the unreleased, but I think

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:19.640
it came out in Japan, the Sega Master System version of the game.

00:37:19.680 --> 00:37:25.160
The only time that the up to jump bothered me was in the Sega Master System version of

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:29.800
the game, and it was because it was just so stiff that it wasn't working.

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:36.740
In the NES version, I actually didn't have a problem jumping and moving in a diagonal sort

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:37.960
of way, like an arch.

00:37:38.080 --> 00:37:41.020
Yeah. I didn't have a problem with that using the NES version.

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:43.600
It was only the Master System version of the game that I found that problem.

00:37:43.600 --> 00:37:46.080
For me, it was there were a few times where you had to jump over an enemy.

00:37:46.420 --> 00:37:50.360
That was my only way around some enemies, and that kind of got a little janky sometimes.

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:52.080
I don't know. I was trying to see the up arrow and the forward arrow.

00:37:52.280 --> 00:37:55.980
I mean, I'm a person that uses up in Smash Brothers, so.

00:37:56.280 --> 00:37:57.360
Oh, yes. Okay.

00:37:57.760 --> 00:37:59.720
I mean, I can live with the up button.

00:38:00.820 --> 00:38:05.440
And then last thing, since we were talking about all the different versions, I will mention

00:38:05.440 --> 00:38:07.640
that this is a great port on the NES here.

00:38:07.640 --> 00:38:13.620
The arcade version doesn't really control that much different, and outside of the graphical

00:38:13.620 --> 00:38:18.220
polish of it, it's pretty much the same game with the levels switched around a little bit.

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:20.280
The order isn't the same, even from the very beginning.

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:25.760
I thought that that was interesting that you're not given the same kind of tutorials, in the

00:38:25.760 --> 00:38:26.660
same order, at least.

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:28.080
Okay. All right.

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:31.760
I think with that, we will take it into the Essential Games List.

00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:37.800
Guys, we're talking about 2018 now.

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:40.500
How does this game hold up in 2018?

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:43.360
I don't care about 1987 anymore.

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:45.420
Is it still 2018? I can't remember.

00:38:45.660 --> 00:38:47.000
Yeah, it's a good question, actually.

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:49.960
Sorry. It could be 2019 by now.

00:38:50.440 --> 00:38:51.740
That's a great question.

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:54.120
We've been in this recording room for a long time.

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:56.080
We've been recording since 10-yard fight.

00:38:56.120 --> 00:38:57.060
What year is it?

00:38:57.060 --> 00:38:58.780
Since 10-yard fight, we've been going.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:03.140
I'm having an existential dread right now, not knowing what year I'm living in.

00:39:03.560 --> 00:39:05.920
But, Mike, save me if you're listening.

00:39:07.840 --> 00:39:13.760
Anyway, so it's 2018, 2019, 2023 when Pat's listening to this.

00:39:15.380 --> 00:39:16.720
Just hope that that happens.

00:39:17.020 --> 00:39:18.380
I think you said Pat last episode.

00:39:18.660 --> 00:39:21.200
Yeah, yeah. The odds would be incredible that Pat listens to both episodes.

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:22.320
These are his first two episodes.

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:23.720
Assuming he wouldn't quit.

00:39:23.840 --> 00:39:25.560
Pat, we mention you on every episode.

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:32.240
Anyway, so it's this year, and you've got to decide if this game's still relevant and still worth playing today.

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:34.920
More importantly, is it still essential?

00:39:35.320 --> 00:39:46.280
Sean. I allowed this game for a lot of just the amount of, like, just the great puzzle design.

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:49.280
This has great puzzle design, but it also has an identity crisis.

00:39:49.280 --> 00:40:02.100
And I think there are a lot of elements of this game that just don't quite fit with the intention, with the intended experience that you as a player are supposed to have.

00:40:02.820 --> 00:40:09.000
You don't have enough time to really experiment with these levels while they beckon for experimentation.

00:40:10.840 --> 00:40:15.860
I think sometimes it gets a little too crowded in those stages.

00:40:16.900 --> 00:40:20.820
I think if you like puzzle games, you should play this game.

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:22.980
It is not on my essential games list.

00:40:23.600 --> 00:40:24.600
All right. There you have it.

00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:32.240
Joe. So I'm not going to repeat all the things Sean just said, because I agree with all the things that he said was wrong with it.

00:40:32.280 --> 00:40:37.480
And I think that's a real shame, because I really think there's a lot of thought put into these puzzles.

00:40:37.660 --> 00:40:40.560
And I think that they're—I mean, obviously, I didn't play a ton of these puzzles.

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:42.180
You said there's 60-some.

00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:43.940
There are 64 total.

00:40:43.940 --> 00:40:58.680
So I think there could probably be some really cool puzzles in this, but all those negative things, particularly the time limit and the action elements that don't quite work, kind of work against it and kind of work against what you want out of a puzzle game.

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:01.580
So I think that's what holds it back for me.

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:03.480
But I would still give it a play.

00:41:03.560 --> 00:41:04.700
I still think it's a lot of fun.

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:08.300
If you can do it within the time limits, that's even better.

00:41:08.460 --> 00:41:10.700
I mean, you probably—it's definitely more of a challenge.

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:15.540
But—and I didn't mention this on the rest of the episode.

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:21.280
Go for it. I also think it would have been great to have a creation mode.

00:41:21.460 --> 00:41:23.360
Oh, creation mode. What do you call that?

00:41:23.580 --> 00:41:25.180
Yeah, like the level builder.

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:27.200
It was a programming series.

00:41:27.480 --> 00:41:29.460
Yes, it was the programming series.

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:33.420
Design mode. Yeah, why couldn't I think of that?

00:41:33.740 --> 00:41:39.540
But yeah, I think that would—like, this is the kind of game that, like, you could make some really cool puzzles and you can't just throw stuff down.

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:40.460
You have to really think about it.

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:42.100
But not essential.

00:41:42.780 --> 00:41:47.120
I would never want to play anybody else's levels.

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:52.220
I only want to—like, just thinking about it, I would only want to play Michitaka's levels.

00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:54.740
Well, it's like—sorry, but I mean, it's like Mario Maker, right?

00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:57.700
No, I know, I know. Like, most of the levels that come out of Mario Maker are terrible.

00:41:57.800 --> 00:42:01.480
Right, right. But, like, if you really want—it's more about making the level that's fun.

00:42:01.620 --> 00:42:06.420
No, I know, I know. And I think that's just me knowing that I couldn't make a good level with this.

00:42:06.420 --> 00:42:11.640
Like, I wouldn't want to play my levels or anyone that, like, you know—NES didn't have online.

00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:14.720
I wasn't going to get the, like, access to someone's cool levels.

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:16.260
That would have just been, like, another feature.

00:42:16.360 --> 00:42:17.280
I would have been like, oh, rats.

00:42:19.020 --> 00:42:21.360
Here's the thing for me with Solomon's Key.

00:42:22.040 --> 00:42:23.640
It's such a unique game.

00:42:23.820 --> 00:42:28.380
It's such a cool puzzle, like, a twist on the puzzle genre, too.

00:42:29.380 --> 00:42:36.720
But for me personally, like, I couldn't get enough enjoyment out of this game because of all of the setbacks.

00:42:36.920 --> 00:42:38.700
The time limit, for one.

00:42:38.840 --> 00:42:42.300
But also just, like, sometimes you're too powerful.

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:45.320
Most of the time you're too weak, in my opinion.

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:48.100
I just—I don't know.

00:42:48.200 --> 00:42:51.480
I kept booting this up, hoping to have a better experience each time.

00:42:51.680 --> 00:42:55.020
But it would fall flat for me pretty fast each time I boot it up.

00:42:55.040 --> 00:42:59.200
So I will say that this is one of my, like, you know, most attempted games.

00:42:59.900 --> 00:43:01.740
I attempted it a lot over, like—

00:43:01.740 --> 00:43:02.800
That's quite disapproletive. Right.

00:43:03.520 --> 00:43:05.720
I attempted it a lot over, like, a two-week period.

00:43:06.080 --> 00:43:10.440
But I can't say that I'll be going back to it now that we're done with this episode.

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:12.680
I don't think this will be a game I'll be hitting up again.

00:43:12.680 --> 00:43:15.020
So I kind of even toss up on the play it.

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:21.120
But I do think if you're, like, a fan of puzzle games in general, this one does something that we haven't really seen.

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:23.160
Iterated a lot.

00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:24.180
So give it a shot.

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:26.160
That's my recommendation.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:35.500
And if you're really a fan of Solomon's Key, you probably know, but just in case you didn't, a sequel was released in 1993 for the NES.

00:43:35.500 --> 00:43:38.220
And we will be doing it on Nostalgia, of course.

00:43:39.040 --> 00:43:40.040
In 11 years.

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:41.360
Yeah. In the U.S.

00:43:41.380 --> 00:43:42.640
it was called Fire and Ice.

00:43:43.060 --> 00:43:47.060
And it is one of the rarest and most expensive NES games.

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:49.240
Oh, my. So that will be a fun purchase.

00:43:51.640 --> 00:43:56.640
But otherwise, it will be a really fun purchase, especially for the people who play along.

00:43:57.000 --> 00:43:58.300
I hope you really like Solomon's Key.

00:43:58.300 --> 00:43:59.820
You might want to get started on that now.

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:01.920
Yeah. You might want to, yeah, start saving.

00:44:02.060 --> 00:44:04.240
Start a 529 plan for that right now.

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:09.640
Otherwise, it was called Solomon's Key 2 outside of America.

00:44:09.800 --> 00:44:11.220
But they didn't want to call it Solomon's Key.

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:12.540
Maybe this game didn't sell well.

00:44:12.540 --> 00:44:21.840
Who knows? Also, I have to throw out that Michitaka has a lot of fun drawings that he did for the game.

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:24.920
And I didn't really want to, like, talk about them.

00:44:24.920 --> 00:44:26.900
But I will definitely post them up on the show notes.

00:44:26.980 --> 00:44:28.100
They're definitely worth checking out.

00:44:28.340 --> 00:44:31.720
He's a very vocal guy who loves to talk about the things that he's doing.

00:44:31.720 --> 00:44:37.000
And he is kind of regarded as, like, one of those early great game designers for both

00:44:37.000 --> 00:44:42.020
Bomb Jack and this and then a bunch of other puzzle games later down the road.

00:44:42.180 --> 00:44:45.540
So I definitely think, like, you know, we'll probably run into him again.

00:44:46.100 --> 00:44:49.820
And I would check out the rest of his stuff if he were jamming on Solomon's Key and Fire

00:44:49.820 --> 00:44:50.660
and Ice back in the day.

00:44:50.740 --> 00:44:53.040
It's definitely worth to see what he continued to make after that.

00:44:53.620 --> 00:44:56.320
But that's all the time we have for this episode of Nostalgia.

